  <div class="tale-introduction"><p>From the <strong>Brihadaranyaka Upanishad</strong>.</p></div>
  <section class="story-characters" aria-labelledby="characters-title"><p class="eyebrow">MEET THE FIGURES</p><h2 id="characters-title">People in the assembly</h2><dl><div><dt>Gargi Vachaknavi</dt><dd>A renowned philosopher whose questions move from the structure of the cosmos to the imperishable reality beneath it.</dd></div><div><dt>Yajnavalkya</dt><dd>A formidable sage whose answers are tested by several learned questioners in Janaka’s court.</dd></div><div><dt>King Janaka of Videha</dt><dd>The royal patron who gathers learned brahmins and offers a prize to the one most deeply versed in sacred knowledge.</dd></div><div><dt>The assembled scholars</dt><dd>Questioners and witnesses whose debate frames the Upanishad’s inquiry into knowledge, self and ultimate reality.</dd></div></dl></section>
  <div class="tale-layout"><aside class="tale-contents"><p class="eyebrow">IN THIS STORY</p><nav aria-label="Story chapters"><a href="#assembly"><span>01</span>Janaka’s assembly</a><a href="#claim"><span>02</span>A bold claim</a><a href="#first-questioning"><span>03</span>Gargi’s first questioning</a><a href="#limit"><span>04</span>The limit of a question</a><a href="#two-arrows"><span>05</span>Two questions like arrows</a><a href="#space"><span>06</span>What is woven in space?</a><a href="#imperishable"><span>07</span>The imperishable</a><a href="#recognition"><span>08</span>Gargi’s recognition</a><a href="#traditions">Text &amp; interpretation</a><a href="/sources.html#gargi-yajnavalkya">Sources &amp; reading notes</a></nav><a class="tale-back" href="/stories.html">Return to the collection</a></aside><article class="tale-body">{% include "reader-tools.njk" %}
    <section class="tale-chapter" id="assembly"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>01</span><h2>Janaka’s assembly</h2></div><p>King Janaka of Videha performs a great sacrifice and gathers learned brahmins from Kuru and Panchala. A thousand cows stand nearby, gold fastened to their horns. Janaka announces that whoever is most deeply learned may take them.</p><p>The prize makes knowledge public and testable. It is not enough to possess a reputation. Whoever steps forward must be ready to answer before the assembly.</p></section>
    <section class="tale-chapter" id="claim"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>02</span><h2>A bold claim</h2></div><p>Yajnavalkya tells his student to drive the cows away. The other scholars object: does he truly claim to be the wisest among them? Yajnavalkya does not offer a speech of self-praise. He says he honours the most learned—but he wants the cows.</p><p>One questioner after another now tests him. The debate travels through ritual, breath, death, self and liberation. Among those who rise is Gargi Vachaknavi.</p></section>
    <section class="tale-chapter" id="first-questioning"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>03</span><h2>Gargi’s first questioning</h2></div><p>Gargi begins with a traditional image: if the world is woven through water like warp and weft, in what is water woven? Yajnavalkya answers, and she continues. Water gives way to air; air to the intermediate regions; these to subtler worlds in an ascending sequence.</p><p>Her inquiry refuses to stop at the first supporting layer. Every answer becomes the ground of a further question: what, in turn, supports this?</p><figure class="tale-inline story-landscape"><img src="/assets/images/gargi-woven-worlds.webp" alt="An imagined Gargi questioning Yajnavalkya beneath an abstract artistic metaphor of woven water, air and luminous sky" width="1536" height="864" loading="lazy"><figcaption>THE WORLDS AS WARP AND WEFT · AI-GENERATED CONCEPTUAL INTERPRETATION</figcaption></figure></section>
    <section class="tale-chapter" id="limit"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>04</span><h2>The limit of a question</h2></div><p>Gargi’s sequence reaches the world of Brahman and asks what lies beyond it. Yajnavalkya warns her not to press the question in a way that treats what is beyond ordinary questioning as one more object in the chain.</p><p>Gargi stops. The moment is severe, and the text’s warning should not be softened into a casual interruption. Yet it is not her final word. After other scholars question Yajnavalkya, Gargi rises again with a more exact approach.</p></section>
    <section class="tale-chapter" id="two-arrows"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>05</span><h2>Two questions like arrows</h2></div><p>Gargi addresses the assembly. She will ask Yajnavalkya two questions, she says, like a warrior of Kashi or Videha stringing a bow and taking two formidable arrows in hand. If he answers them, none present will defeat him in a debate about Brahman.</p><p>She asks what is above heaven, below earth and between them—what includes what people call past, present and future. In what is all this woven, warp and weft?</p></section>
    <section class="tale-chapter" id="space"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>06</span><h2>What is woven in space?</h2></div><p>Yajnavalkya answers: all this is woven in <em>akasha</em>—space or the vast expanse. Gargi acknowledges the answer, then releases her second question.</p><p>In what, then, is akasha itself woven? The question no longer asks for another visible region or named cosmic world. It asks for that upon which even the field of all appearances depends.</p></section>
    <section class="tale-chapter" id="imperishable"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>07</span><h2>The imperishable</h2></div><p>Yajnavalkya answers that knowers of Brahman call it the Imperishable—the <em>akshara</em>. His teaching proceeds by refusing every easy category: it is neither gross nor subtle, neither short nor long, without the ordinary marks by which objects are grasped.</p><p>Yet this is not mere emptiness. Under the governance of the Imperishable, the sun and moon remain in their courses, heaven and earth hold their places, moments and seasons unfold, and rivers move from their sources. It is the unseen seer, the unheard hearer, the unthought thinker and the unknown knower. Nothing else stands behind it as another seer or knower.</p></section>
    <section class="tale-chapter" id="recognition"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>08</span><h2>Gargi’s recognition</h2></div><p>Gargi turns to the assembled brahmins. They should consider themselves fortunate if they can depart after offering Yajnavalkya respect, she tells them; none of them will defeat him in debate concerning Brahman.</p><p>Her recognition is not surrender to status. It follows a public test conducted with precision. Gargi’s role is indispensable: by refusing shallow closure, she draws the discussion from layered cosmology toward the Upanishad’s teaching on the imperishable ground of experience.</p></section>
    <section class="tale-reflection" id="reflection"><p class="eyebrow">EDITORIAL REFLECTION / PAUSE &amp; REFLECT</p><h2>What remains when<br><em>every support is questioned?</em></h2><p>Gargi does not ask only for more information. She tests whether each explanation rests upon something still deeper. Which of your own assumptions have become invisible because you have never asked what supports them? This is an editorial invitation, not a translated verse.</p></section>
    <section class="tale-reflection" id="traditions"><p class="eyebrow">TEXT &amp; INTERPRETATION</p><h2>A debate in two movements.<br><em>Not one continuous exchange.</em></h2><p>This reader preserves the distinction between Gargi’s first questioning in 3.6 and her later two questions in 3.8. Other learned interlocutors question Yajnavalkya between these passages. The page follows the supplied Gita Press edition with Shankara’s commentary while recognising that Vedanta traditions interpret Brahman, akshara and the relation of self and ultimate reality differently.</p><p>The court, clothing and faces in the illustrations are imagined. The woven sky is a conceptual metaphor, not a literal diagram from the text. Brahman and the Imperishable are deliberately not personified.</p><a href="/sources.html#gargi-yajnavalkya">Read the passage map and edition notes</a></section>
